course: euphemisms
lesson: 25
title: Soft words for hard things: politics and government spin and war, the military and violence
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: War and violence are the hardest things of all to talk about, so governments, armies and criminals give them cold, technical names.
explain: A word like "neutralize" or "eliminate" sounds like tidying up, but in a soldier's or a spy's report it means to kill.
explain: Politics does the same with money and bad news: a statement that turned out false is suddenly called "inoperative".
explain: These words put distance between people and what is really done, which is why it matters to know what they mean.
explain: When you hear them, translate them back: kill, prison, riot, propaganda, false.
fact: "Earmarks" means money set aside for a politician's pet local projects, favourite schemes in his own area (US).
fact: "Soft money" means political donations (gifts of money) that dodge spending limits, getting round the rules on how much may be spent (US).
fact: "Inoperative" said of an earlier statement means it was false (US).
fact: "A good day to bury bad news" means a day when bad news will go unnoticed because something bigger is happening (UK).
fact: "Civil unrest" means riots and protests.
fact: "Disturbances" means riots (UK).
fact: "Emergency measures" can mean that normal rights and laws are suspended (stopped for a while).
fact: "Messaging" means propaganda or spin.
fact: "Friendly fire" means an attack on one's own side by mistake.
fact: "Neutralize" in military talk means kill.
fact: "Take out" means kill or destroy.
fact: "Eliminate" means kill.
fact: "Liquidate" means kill, especially many people; dictators (rulers with total power who cannot be voted out) have used it.
fact: "Terminate with extreme prejudice" means assassinate, murder an important or chosen person on purpose (US spy slang).
fact: "Wet work" means assassination (slang).
fact: "Enhanced interrogation" means torture (US).
fact: "Extraordinary rendition" means secretly moving a prisoner to another country for interrogation, long hard questioning (US).
fact: "Black site" means a secret prison (US).
fact: "Detainee" means a prisoner, often held without trial (never judged in a court).
fact: "Enemy combatant" means a prisoner denied the rights of a prisoner of war, a captured soldier protected by the rules of war (US).
exercise: What are "earmarks" in US politics? | Money set aside for a politician's pet local projects | Marks made on a dog's ears by the vet to show its owner | Notes in a book's margin | Fines for loud music
exercise: What is "soft money" in US elections? | Donations that dodge spending limits | Coins made of soft metal | Money that is easy to spend | Paper money only
exercise: What does it mean when a spokesman calls an earlier statement "inoperative"? | It was false | It was switched off by mistake | It was too long | It was in a foreign language
exercise: What does "a good day to bury bad news" mean? | A day when bad news will go unnoticed | A day for a funeral in the rain at the church | A day with no news at all | A day to plant a garden
exercise: What does "civil unrest" mean in the news? | Riots and protests | People being polite | A quiet day in town | A busy shopping day
exercise: What does a UK report mean by "disturbances" in a city? | Riots | Loud music at night | Fog | Bad weather
exercise: What can "emergency measures" mean when a government declares them? | Normal rights and laws are suspended | Ambulances get new sirens | A first aid course for all | Rulers to measure floods
exercise: What does "messaging" mean in politics? | Propaganda or spin | Sending text messages | Writing letters to friends | Delivering the post
exercise: What is "friendly fire"? | An attack on one's own side by mistake | A cosy fire in a camp where soldiers sit and chat | Fireworks at a party | Shots fired only to warn
exercise: What does "neutralize" mean in a military report about an enemy? | Kill | Make calm and friendly | Halt | Make him change sides
exercise: What does "take out" mean in a soldier's report about a target? | Kill or destroy | Take for a meal | Remove from a list | Carry to safety
exercise: What does "eliminate" mean when a gang or army says it of a person? | Kill | Remove from a football game | Send home from school | Crop
exercise: What does "liquidate" mean when a dictator says it of people? | Kill, especially many people | Melt them into liquid | Sell their shops cheaply | Pay all their debts
exercise: What does "terminate with extreme prejudice" mean in US spy slang? | Assassinate | Fire with a bad reference | End a contract early | Stop a bus very suddenly
exercise: What is "wet work" in criminal or spy slang? | Assassination | Cleaning windows | Washing dishes | Working in the rain
exercise: What was "enhanced interrogation" really? | Torture | A longer interview | A friendly chat with tea | A quiz
exercise: What is "extraordinary rendition"? | Secretly moving a prisoner to another country for interrogation | A very good song performance by a famous singer at a big concert | A special drawing of a building | A fast delivery service
exercise: What is a "black site"? | A secret prison | A website with a dark theme | A coal mine | A burnt-down building
exercise: What does "detainee" usually mean? | A prisoner, often held without trial | A pupil kept in after school | A traveller whose train is late | A guest staying at a hotel
exercise: What did "enemy combatant" mean for prisoners held by the US? | A prisoner denied a prisoner of war's rights | A soldier who won a medal for bravery in battle | A player on the other team | A spy who changed sides
test: News: "Three soldiers were killed by friendly fire." Who fired the shots? | Their own side, by mistake | The enemy, on purpose | Nobody, it was an accident with a fire | A friendly neighbour
test: "they said the target was neutralized, mate, that means dead, right?" — is the speaker right? | Yes, it means killed | No, it means calmed down | No, it means captured | No, it means moved away
test: A government spokesman: "The minister's earlier statement is now inoperative." What does this admit? | The earlier statement was false | The minister's microphone broke | The statement was too long | The minister has left
test: A leaked email says: "Today is a good day to bury bad news." What is the writer planning? | To release bad news when nobody will notice | To hold a funeral for an old friend from work | To bury a time capsule | To cancel the news
test: Report: "Police were called to disturbances in the city centre." What happened? | There were riots | There was loud music | There was a traffic jam | It was raining
test: "they called it enhanced interrogation, but it was torture, plain and simple" — what is the speaker saying? | The soft name hides torture | It was a long interview | It was a friendly chat | It was a quiz
test: The news says a man was taken by "extraordinary rendition" to a "black site". What happened to him? | He was secretly moved to a secret prison | He went to a big concert in the city | He moved to a new house | He visited a mine
test: "the gang wanted him eliminated, that's how bad it got" — what did the gang want? | Him killed | Him out of a game | Him sent home | Him left out of a photo
test: Mia says: "Liquidate means turn into water, so the dictator melted the farmers?" Is she right? | No, it means he killed them | Yes, it means melted | Yes, it means watered their fields | No, it means he paid them
test: The campaign's "messaging" was criticised. What was criticised? | Its spin or propaganda | Its text messages | Its post office | Its phone bills
test: "Emergency measures were declared and the courts were closed." What has happened? | Normal rights and laws were suspended | A first aid course started | New ambulances arrived | Floods were measured
test: A senator is accused of adding "earmarks" to the budget. What is he accused of? | Setting money aside for pet local projects | Marking the pages with his ears | Cutting the budget | Signing the budget late
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the term "friendly fire" as employed in military reports. | An attack inflicted upon one's own forces in error | A fire lit for warmth by allied troops | Shots discharged solely as a warning | A ceremonial salute by an ally
exam: It is given that an official describes a former statement as "inoperative". Select the response consistent with the established usage of the term. | The former statement was untrue | The former statement was inaudible | The former statement was translated | The former statement was abbreviated
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice termed "enhanced interrogation"? | It is a euphemism for torture | It is a lengthier interview conducted with courtesy | It is a test administered by machine | It is a lawful examination before a court
exam: Ascertain the plain meaning of "black site" as employed in reports concerning the detention of prisoners. | A secret prison | A website of dark design | A disused coal mine | A building destroyed by fire
